Save a compiled Curry module
============================

This example writes the compiled form of a Curry module to a script with
curry.save, and then runs that script.  Saving works on the C++ backend
and the reference backend.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

The module wildcard_matching.curry matches a string against a pattern with
the wildcards ? and *.  The function tails is non-deterministic: it returns
every suffix of a string.  The matcher calls a set function, set2 aux, on
the string and the pattern, and tests whether the set of results is empty.
The goal main checks ten cases and is True when every case agrees.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The script go.py imports the module and saves it:

    curry.save(wildcard_matching, 'wildcard_matching.py', main='main')

The saved file is a script that embeds the backend object of the module.
The goal argument records the goal to evaluate.  When the script runs, it
imports the Sprite runtime, loads the module, and prints every value of
main.  The script reads -g as sprite-exec does: -g NAME evaluates another
goal of the module. Omit main to save a module for curry.load instead.

The saved module belongs to the current backend. A saved program embeds
that object and runs through the Python API of the installation. This
example uses the backend selected by SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS or the
installed default.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script runs go.py and then the saved script, wildcard_matching.py.
The saved script is a build product and is not checked in.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the output that ./run is meant to produce:

    % python go.py
    Loading and compiling wildcard_matching.curry
    Saving the program to wildcard_matching.py with goal main
    % python wildcard_matching.py
    True

The last line is the value of main.


5. Limits
---------

A warm run of ./run took 0.8 s.  The first run also compiles the module
with the Curry front end, which takes about half a second more.
